6. The suit was contested by the defendant No. 1 alone by tiling a written statement denying the case of the plaint and raising various defence to the claim including the claim that it was barred by limitation. Main defence is however that there was no privacy of contract between the plaintiff and the defendant and as such, the suit at the instance of the plaintiff is not maintainable. It was also claimed that no insurance policy was issued in favour of the plaintiff by the defendant. The survey findings of the Surveyors are not binding unless the risk of the loss is covered by a valid insurance policy. The property allegedly surveyed is also not covered by any validly issued policy. 

 a. the suit as framed is not barred by limitation; 

 13. The suit was contested by the defendant No. 1 alone by filing a written statement denying the case of the plaint. They contended that the claim of the plaintiff was false, barred both under limitation and insurance law. In the report, the surveyors mentioned that due to the leakage of the electric wire lying on the bales of cotton in the godown when the fire occurred. Absence of fixed electric wiring in the godown was the reason for the fire. This was a violation of the terms of the contract of insurance. They rejected the claim. 

 a. the suit as framed is not barred by limitation; 

 20. The suit was contested by the defendant No. 1 alone by filing a written statement denying the case of the plaint. They contended that the claim of the plaintiff is false, barred both under limitation and insurance law. Cotton were kept in the godown in sacks. Fire were occurred in those sacks. This was a violation of the terms of the contract of insurance. They rejected the claim. 

 a. the suit as framed is not barred by limitation; 

 28. We have read the pleadings of the parties and the evidence on record, oral and documentary and the judgments. The facts have now come out to be straight and admit of no dispute. The goods lost by fire were insured with and covered by the policies issued by the defendant No. 1. Two surveyors appointed by the defendant No. 1 jointly surveyed and found the goods in all the three suits were covered by the policies. The surveyors also assessed the loss as stated above. The loss suffered was during the currency of the policies. All the suits were instituted within the period of limitation. The defendant No. 1 was obliged under each policy to pay the claims within 90 days of the assessment of loss but they did not settle the clams. The main defence raised against the claims of the plaintiffs is that there was no contract with the plaintiff and that the plaintiff is not the insured in the policies. The suits at the instance of the plaintiffs for claims under the policies are therefore, not maintainable.
